const projects={
  "radar": {
    "label": "RESEARCH / LAWRENCE BERKELEY NATIONAL LABORATORY",
    "title": "Atmospheric radar research at Berkeley Lab",
    "technical": `<p class="detail-lead">Repurposing a TI AWR2944 77–81 GHz automotive FMCW radar into a low-power atmospheric sensor: configurable waveforms, autonomous acquisition, custom TLV processing, weak-return enhancement, and remote deployment.</p>
<div class="radar-case-map"><a href="#radar-problem"><span>01</span><strong>Sensing problem</strong><small>Why atmospheric returns are difficult</small></a><a href="#radar-front-end"><span>02</span><strong>Radar front end</strong><small>Waveforms, range, Doppler, resolution</small></a><a href="#radar-control"><span>03</span><strong>Autonomous control</strong><small>Pi, Feather, RTC, relay, storage</small></a><a href="#radar-dsp"><span>04</span><strong>Acquisition & data pipeline</strong><small>Configuration, capture, processing</small></a><a href="#radar-weak"><span>05</span><strong>Reflector & detection</strong><small>Long-range test, RCS, CFAR</small></a><a href="#radar-field"><span>06</span><strong>Field system</strong><small>Power, reliability, deployment</small></a></div>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-problem"><p class="chapter-number">01 / THE SENSING PROBLEM</p><h3>Atmospheric sensing goals</h3><p class="radar-problem-intro">Our goal is to adapt automotive mmWave radar for frequent, distributed observations of precipitation and other processes in the atmospheric boundary layer.</p><div class="radar-problem-grid"><div class="radar-problem-panel radar-platform-choice"><h4>Why automotive radar?</h4><p><b>Low cost and low power consumption</b> make these compact sensors attractive for deploying multiple instruments and supporting long field experiments with a limited power budget.</p></div><div class="radar-problem-panel"><h4>The sensing challenge</h4><p>Atmospheric returns can be much weaker than vehicle reflections. Detecting them requires separating faint echoes from receiver noise and stationary ground or structural clutter.</p></div></div><div class="radar-project-focus"><p class="radar-focus-label">MY ENGINEERING FOCUS</p><p>Configure the radar, improve weak-return processing, and build reliable power and acquisition control for unattended measurements.</p></div></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-front-end"><p class="chapter-number">02 / FMCW FOUNDATIONS</p><h3>FMCW radar fundamentals</h3><p>In the months before joining the research team, I studied mmWave radar and FMCW signal processing. The figures below summarize the signal chain, range and velocity estimation, and measurement limits that formed the foundation for my contributions to radar configuration and data processing.</p><div class="radar-theory-gallery" aria-label="FMCW radar signal chain and measurement theory"><figure class="detail-figure radar-theory-chain"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-signal-chain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW signal-chain diagram at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-signal-chain.svg" alt="FMCW transmitter: ramp generator, local oscillator, power amplifier, and TX antenna. Receiver: RX antenna, low-noise amplifier, mixer, IF stage, ADC, and DSP." width="1200" height="510" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>How FMCW radar works.</b> Transmitting, receiving, and processing the radar signal.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-mixing.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW beat-frequency and chirp slide at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-mixing-preview.webp" alt="Lecture slide showing signed beat-frequency equations for up and down chirps and the relationships to target range and Doppler shift." width="960" height="540" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>Calculating range and velocity.</b> Using the transmitted and received chirps to find distance and motion.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-limits.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW range and velocity resolution slide at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-limits-preview.webp" alt="Lecture slide relating range resolution to bandwidth and velocity resolution to wavelength, chirp count, and chirp repetition period." width="960" height="540" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>Range and velocity limits.</b> Calculating resolution and maximum measurable range and velocity.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="fine radar-theory-credit">Theory slides: Stijn Wielandt, <em>mmWave Radar Applications for Science</em>, slides 12–13.</p></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-control"><p class="chapter-number">03 / AUTONOMOUS HARDWARE & POWER</p><h3>Autonomous control hardware</h3><p>The Feather MCU forms a separate supervisory layer. Its deterministic run/idle/recovery state machine uses a FeatherWing RTC and 1 Hz timing for scheduled wake windows. A latching relay lets the controller switch power to the Raspberry Pi and radar during long idle periods or recovery. This division means a stalled Pi does not have to recover itself.</p><h3>A compact Adafruit/Raspberry Pi assembly</h3><p>I integrated an Adafruit MCU and its peripherals with the Raspberry Pi using an Adafruit/Raspberry Pi HAT. Stacking the boards keeps the control hardware and its connections together in a compact assembly.</p><div class="radar-integration-pair"><figure class="detail-figure integration-photo"><a href="assets/adafruit-raspi-stack-portrait.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/adafruit-raspi-stack-portrait.svg" alt="Adafruit MCU and peripheral boards stacked compactly with a Raspberry Pi through an Adafruit/Raspberry Pi HAT" width="1500" height="2000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Adafruit MCU and peripherals integrated with the Raspberry Pi in a compact HAT-based stack.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure detail-video integration-video"><video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/adafruit-peripherals-poster.jpg" aria-label="Silent portrait video of Hashem working on Adafruit peripheral connections"><source src="assets/adafruit-peripherals.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Working on the Adafruit peripheral connections.</span><button type="button" class="text-link" data-video-toggle>Play video</button></figcaption></figure></div><section class="radar-power-section" aria-labelledby="radar-power-title"><h3 id="radar-power-title">Relay control and regulated power</h3><div class="radar-power-layout"><div class="radar-power-copy"><p>The controller switches power independently of the Raspberry Pi, managing scheduled wake windows and restarting the acquisition hardware when needed.</p><p>I use DC–DC buck converters to step down and regulate the supply. When the relay reconnects the radar, they provide stable operating voltages for startup and data acquisition.</p><div class="radar-power-sequence" aria-label="Power sequence"><span>Scheduled wake</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Relay on</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Regulated power</span></div></div><div class="radar-power-media"><figure class="detail-figure detail-video relay-demo"><video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/relay-demo-poster.jpg" aria-label="Silent bench video of the relay power-control prototype"><source src="assets/relay-demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Relay switching prototype.</span><button type="button" class="text-link" data-video-toggle>Play video</button></figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure radar-power-figure"><a href="assets/radar-regulated-power.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/radar-regulated-power.jpg" alt="DC–DC converters wired to the relay-controlled Raspberry Pi and Adafruit controller assembly" width="2361" height="2333" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>DC–DC converters and regulated power wiring.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-dsp"><p class="chapter-number">04 / ACQUISITION, PROCESSING & PACKETIZATION</p><h3>Radar control and data acquisition</h3><p>The Raspberry Pi does more than collect serial bytes. At boot it opens separate CLI and data UART paths, discovers configuration files, sends the selected command sequence to the radar, starts and stops each acquisition, names and records the binary stream, copies the configuration beside the measurement, and writes status logs. Keeping the exact configuration with the data makes each run traceable.</p><div class="code-evidence-grid"><figure class="detail-figure"><div class="radar-code-crop"><img src="assets/radar-pi-code.jpg" alt="Raspberry Pi Python acquisition code showing configuration upload, sensor start, serial data recording, and sensor stop" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The acquisition loop uploads a configuration, timestamps the output, issues <code>sensorStart</code>, reads the data UART, appends binary packets, and guarantees <code>sensorStop</code> on completion or interruption.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/radar-run-log.jpg" alt="Raspberry Pi run log showing sensor stop, stored data size, and experiment end time" loading="lazy"><figcaption>End-of-run logging records the stop command, 1,320,000-byte capture, and completion timestamp.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Radar data processing and packetization</h3><div class="radar-cube-layout"><figure class="detail-figure radar-cube-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-cube-simple.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the radar cube figure at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cube-simple.svg" alt="Radar cube with range, Doppler, and channel dimensions. Samples within each chirp support range processing; successive chirps support velocity processing; antenna channels support direction estimation." width="1200" height="760" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption>Organizing radar data by samples, chirps, and antenna channels.</figcaption></figure><div class="radar-cube-copy"><h4>Understanding the radar cube</h4><p>Each frame groups samples within a chirp, successive chirps, and antenna channels. A <b>range FFT</b> acts on samples within each chirp to resolve distance. A <b>Doppler FFT</b> acts across chirps to resolve radial velocity. Phase differences between antenna channels can then support direction estimation.</p><h4>Parsing the radar output</h4><p>The radar sends selected processed results in Type–Length–Value (TLV) packets. My Python parser validates the frames and reshapes the payloads into NumPy arrays, including range–Doppler maps and range or noise profiles. These are processed outputs, rather than the raw ADC cube.</p><h4>Packetization and spatial mapping</h4><p>After processing, I group and packetize recordings from all radars with their <b>radar ID, configuration ID, reference tower</b> at Jornada Experimental Range, <b>radar coordinates, and orientation</b>. Each recording stays linked to the settings active during acquisition, including range resolution. Together, these details let us place its range bins along the radar’s viewing direction and identify which part of the atmosphere was sampled.</p></div></div><div class="pipeline-diagram radar-cube-pipeline" aria-label="Radar data output pipeline"><span>Configure + capture</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Radar processing</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>TLV parsing</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Packetization + metadata</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Spatial mapping</span></div></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-weak"><p class="chapter-number">05 / REFLECTOR TEST & DETECTION</p><section class="reflector-experiment" aria-labelledby="reflector-test-title"><p class="eyebrow">LONG-RANGE REFLECTOR TEST</p><h4 id="reflector-test-title">Long-range reflector test</h4><p>We set up a spinning corner reflector <strong>175 m from the radar</strong> to explore long-range target visibility with the TI AWR2944. The paired animations show the outdoor setup and the radar’s range–Doppler display.</p><div class="reflector-animation-group" data-gif-pair><div class="reflector-animations"><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/reflector-setup-still.jpg" data-gif-src="assets/reflector-setup.gif" data-still-src="assets/reflector-setup-still.jpg" alt="Outdoor spinning-corner-reflector test setup" width="319" height="568" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Outdoor reflector setup.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/reflector-display-still.jpg" data-gif-src="assets/reflector-display.gif" data-still-src="assets/reflector-display-still.jpg" alt="Range–Doppler display during the spinning-reflector experiment" width="319" height="568" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Inspecting the range–Doppler return.</figcaption></figure></div><button type="button" class="text-link" data-gif-toggle aria-pressed="true">Play animations</button></div><div class="reflector-rcs"><h5>How reflective is a 5 cm corner?</h5><p>For an ideal conducting <strong>square trihedral</strong> with interior edge <em>a</em> = 0.05 m, aligned for peak return:</p><p class="reflector-equation">λ = c/f &nbsp; · &nbsp; σ<sub>peak</sub> ≈ 12πa<sup>4</sup>/λ<sup>2</sup></p><p>At the 79 GHz band center, λ ≈ <strong>3.795 mm</strong>, giving σ<sub>peak</sub> ≈ <strong>16.36 m² (12.14 dBsm)</strong>. Across 77–81 GHz, the estimate ranges from <strong>15.54 to 17.20 m²</strong> (11.92–12.36 dBsm).</p><p class="fine">This is a calculated peak RCS. The return varies as the reflector spins; alignment, construction, and ground reflections affect the observed response. The 175 m separation is the test distance.</p><p class="reference-line">References: <a href="https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc902372/m2/1/high_res_d/929123.pdf#page=12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sandia · square-trihedral model, Eq. 4 ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.ti.com/product/AWR2944" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TI AWR2944 ↗</a></p></div></section><section class="radar-cfar-analysis" id="radar-cfar" aria-labelledby="radar-cfar-title"><p class="eyebrow">CFAR & DETECTION</p><h4 id="radar-cfar-title">CFAR detection</h4><p>I use CFAR (constant false alarm rate) to estimate the local noise floor around regions of interest in the range–Doppler map. Nearby reference cells provide the background estimate, while guard cells help keep the target’s return out of that estimate. This sets an adaptive detection threshold that follows the local background, helping distinguish potential target returns from noise.</p><div class="radar-cfar-pair"><figure class="detail-figure"><a href="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the original range–Doppler power map at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" alt="Original range–Doppler power heatmap before CFAR detection" width="924" height="390" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><b>Before CFAR.</b> Measured power across range and radial velocity. <a href="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full size ↗</a></figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a href="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the binary CFAR detection mask at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" alt="CFAR range–Doppler detection mask with a Detection color scale labeled 0 and 1" width="920" height="390" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><b>After CFAR.</b> Cells marked by the detector, shown on a binary Detection scale. <a href="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full size ↗</a></figcaption></figure></div><figure class="detail-figure radar-cfar-code"><div class="radar-cfar-crop"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-workbench.jpg" alt="Cropped notebook workstation view showing original heatmap, background estimate, and detection mask beside Python CFAR code" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption><b>The implementation.</b> The Python CFAR code sits beside its diagnostic plots, making it easier to inspect how the background estimate leads to the detection mask.</figcaption></figure></section></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-field"><p class="chapter-number">06 / INTEGRATION, POWER & FIELD DEPLOYMENT</p><h3>Hardware integration and field deployment</h3><p>The integrated enclosure combines the radar, conductive-PLA parabolic reflector, Raspberry Pi, Feather controller, relay, external storage, and regulated power stages. The reflector narrows the effective field of view for beam-focused measurements. The controller coordinates power sequencing and wake windows; the Pi manages experiment state, radar configuration, data files, and logs.</p><figure class="detail-figure radar-wide"><img src="assets/radar-integrated.jpg" alt="Integrated atmospheric radar enclosure with reflector, radar board, Raspberry Pi, and control electronics" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Integrated radar, reflector, compute, control, and power distribution.</figcaption></figure><div class="result-pair three"><div><strong>~5.3 W</strong><span>reported average system power</span></div><div><strong>1.37 GB/day</strong><span>reported data rate per radar</span></div><div><strong>Up to 6 months</strong><span>estimated 256 GB recording duration</span></div></div><p>The CASIS 2026 poster reports reliable startup/configuration sequencing, stable power management, and long-duration visibility through remote status reporting. It documents radar system #1 deployed at Jornada Experimental Range, New Mexico, as of June 2, 2026. Ten units were scheduled for deployment to observe dust storms and summer monsoon rainfall; that schedule is a future-work statement, not a claim that all ten were already operating.</p><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/field.webp" alt="Atmospheric radar system deployed at Jornada Experimental Range in New Mexico" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Field system at Jornada Experimental Range, New Mexico.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure casis-event-photo"><a href="assets/casis-event.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/casis-event.jpg" alt="Group photograph of participants at a CASIS event" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>CASIS event group photo.</figcaption></figure><p class="fine">Reported system metrics and deployment status come from the collaborative CASIS 2026 poster by Hashem Taleb, Jeffrey Kwan, Howard Yao, William Henry, Ivo Marković, and Stijn Wielandt.</p><a class="button" href="assets/research-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the CASIS poster ↗</a></section>`,
    "storyTitle": "My time at LBNL",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I joined Berkeley Lab in January 2026 and have spent roughly 10 months working with my mentor, Stijn Wielandt.</p><h3>From internships to an ongoing role</h3><p>During that time, I completed two 10-week, full-time internships and continued as an undergraduate research assistant between those periods. I currently work with the team alongside my studies at UC Berkeley.</p><p>Returning to the same research over several parts of the year has given me time to stay involved as the radar system develops. My experience has moved between software, hardware integration, and outdoor testing, with Stijn’s mentorship connecting those parts of the work.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lab-portrait.jpg?v=8651\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lab-portrait.jpg?v=8651\" alt=\"Hashem with radar hardware at Berkeley Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Working with the radar system during my time at Berkeley Lab.</figcaption></figure><h3>Outdoor validation</h3><p>This nighttime test brought the full measurement chain outdoors: the tripod-mounted radar, live laptop interface, configuration workflow, and operator checks all ran together in the intended sensing geometry.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/outdoor-testing.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/outdoor-testing.jpg\" alt=\"Hashem operating a tripod-mounted atmospheric radar with a laptop during a nighttime outdoor test at Berkeley Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Nighttime end-to-end radar validation on the Berkeley Lab hillside.</figcaption></figure><h3>Visiting the Advanced Light Source</h3><p>A visit to Berkeley Lab’s Advanced Light Source offered a closer look at another large-scale scientific instrument and the people and infrastructure behind research across the laboratory.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" alt=\"Group visit inside Berkeley Lab’s Advanced Light Source facility\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Visiting the Advanced Light Source with fellow Berkeley Lab researchers and students.</figcaption></figure><h3>Photos from Berkeley Lab</h3><p>The photos capture a few parts of those months: working with the equipment, testing outdoors, visiting the ALS, meeting Mike Witherell, then Berkeley Lab’s director, and the view across the bay. Alongside the technical project, they give a sense of the place where I have spent much of this year.</p><div class=\"detail-photo-pair\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lbnl-director.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lbnl-director.jpg\" alt=\"Hashem with Mike Witherell, then Berkeley Lab director, and a colleague\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>With Mike Witherell, then director of Berkeley Lab.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/sunset.jpg\" alt=\"View across the Bay Area from the laboratory\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>A view across the bay from the lab.</figcaption></figure></div>"
  },
  "inverter": {
    "label": "POWER ELECTRONICS / JUL–AUG 2025",
    "title": "Discrete H-bridge inverter",
    "technical": `<p class="detail-lead">A breadboarded DC-to-AC inverter using IRF4905 PMOS and IRFZ44N NMOS devices, discrete BJT control, and microcontroller timing.</p>
<div class="inverter-hero-grid">
  <figure class="detail-figure detail-video inverter-demo"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/inverter-demo-poster.jpg" aria-label="Inverter powering anti-parallel red and blue LEDs"><source src="assets/inverter-demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Proof of operation: the opposing LEDs respond to opposite output polarities.</span><button class="text-link" type="button" data-video-toggle>Pause video</button></figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="detail-figure schematic-feature"><a href="assets/inverter-final-schematic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/inverter-final-schematic.jpg" alt="LTspice H-bridge schematic with two NMOS driver capacitors and a 16 V supply" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Topology reference: two NMOS driver capacitors. This simulation screenshot uses a 16 V supply.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<h3>Inverter testing</h3><p>The anti-parallel red and blue LEDs provide a direct polarity check: one is forward-biased for one bridge polarity and the other for the reverse polarity. This establishes a bipolar output at the load; it does not alone establish a sinusoidal waveform, RMS voltage, current capacity, or efficiency.</p>
<h3>Revised switching logic</h3><div class="detail-skills"><div><h4>High-side PMOS</h4><p>Pulling the gate toward ground makes V<sub>GS</sub> negative and turns the device on. Returning the gate to its source turns it off.</p></div><div><h4>Low-side NMOS</h4><p>A positive V<sub>GS</sub> turns the device on. The revised topology retains the two NMOS capacitor gate-driver circuits.</p></div><div><h4>Complementary leg drive</h4><p>Each leg commands the PMOS and NMOS oppositely, reducing simultaneous conduction through the rail.</p></div><div><h4>Output reversal</h4><p>Alternating diagonal device pairs reverses the voltage across the load.</p></div></div>
<p class="engineering-note"><strong>Design caveat.</strong> Complementary commands reduce shoot-through risk, but real hardware still needs dead-time and oscilloscope verification because device and driver delays are unequal.</p>
`,
    "storyTitle": "From four gate drivers to the 20 V prototype",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I started with a separate capacitor gate-driver circuit for each MOSFET, then simplified the PMOS drive when I chose a 20 V input.</p>\n<div class=\"iteration-list\">\n<article><span>01 / FIRST PROTOTYPE</span><div><h3>One gate driver per MOSFET</h3><p>The first H-bridge had four capacitor gate-driver circuits: one for each of the two PMOS and two NMOS devices.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-four-cap.jpg\" alt=\"First breadboard H-bridge with four capacitor gate-driver circuits, one per MOSFET\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>First prototype: four MOSFETs and four gate-driver capacitors.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n<article><span>02 / 20 V INPUT</span><div><h3>Simplifying the PMOS gate drive</h3><p>When I decided to use a 20 V input, I removed the capacitor gate-driver setup for the two PMOS devices. I also removed the other two BJTs to improve efficiency. The revised PMOS drive pulls each gate toward ground to turn it on and returns it toward its source to turn it off. The two NMOS capacitor gate-driver circuits remained.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-two-cap.jpg\" alt=\"Revised breadboard H-bridge with two gate-driver capacitors remaining\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Revised prototype: PMOS capacitor gate drivers removed; two NMOS driver capacitors retained.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n<article><span>03 / LAST HARDWARE ITERATION</span><div><h3>A 16 V capacitor at 20 V</h3><p>In the last hardware iteration, I applied 20 V across a capacitor rated for 16 V. That exceeded its voltage rating and caused it to break down. The photo shows the H-bridge afterward, with one intact gate-driver capacitor remaining.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-failure.jpg\" alt=\"Last breadboard H-bridge iteration after capacitor breakdown, with one intact electrolytic gate-driver capacitor remaining\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Last hardware iteration, after a 16 V capacitor broke down under 20 V.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n</div>"
  },
  "oscilloscope": {
    "label": "ANALOG & DIGITAL HARDWARE / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "Two-channel oscilloscope",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I’m developing a two-channel oscilloscope with a team. Our goal is to follow a signal all the way from the probe input to digital samples on a computer, bringing analog conditioning, high-speed conversion, and FPGA interfacing into one instrument.</p>\n<p>We are working through the circuit design and PCB integration. The schematics below show how the stages fit together; board bring-up and measured performance validation are still ahead.</p>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-analog-driver\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">01 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-analog-driver\">Probe front end and differential ADC driver</h3><p>The probe signal passes through input protection and the OPA140/BUF802 stages before reaching the LMH6881 differential driver. Its two output branches are filtered and routed to ADC_P and ADC_N, the positive and negative inputs of one ADC channel. Offset and common-mode connections set the signal’s DC operating point, while SPI control allows the driver gain to be adjusted.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-analog-driver.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-analog-driver.webp\" alt=\"Probe front end schematic showing OPA140, BUF802, LMH6881, output filters, and differential ADC_P and ADC_N signals\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>Analog_FE_Channel.SchDoc · PDF page 10</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-analog-driver.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-dac-offset\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">02 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-dac-offset\">DAC and programmable DC offset</h3><p>The SPI-controlled DAC80502 provides a separate programmable output for each channel. OPA2197 inverting stages turn those outputs into OFFSET_CH1 and OFFSET_CH2, allowing the analog front ends’ DC offsets to be adjusted. The same reference network supplies filtered, TLV9062-buffered reference outputs and a 1.25 V common-mode reference. These connections establish the analog operating points around which the signal varies.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-dac-offset.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-dac-offset.webp\" alt=\"DAC80502 dual-channel DC offset schematic with SPI control, OPA2197 offset stages, TLV9062 reference buffers, and common-mode reference divider\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>Analog_Offset.SchDoc · PDF page 9</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-dac-offset.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-adc-clock\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">03 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-adc-clock\">ADC and external 250 MHz sampling clock</h3><p>The ADS4229 takes the conditioned differential signals from both channels and converts them into digital samples. An external SiT9365 250 MHz oscillator drives CLKP and CLKM through the clock network, establishing when sampling occurs. The ADC sends data and an accompanying clock toward the FPGA over LVDS connections; SPI provides configuration access, and local decoupling supports the analog and digital supply rails.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-adc-clock.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-adc-clock.webp\" alt=\"ADS4229 ADC schematic with two differential analog input channels, external SiT9365 250 MHz sampling oscillator, SPI control, and LVDS outputs\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>Analog_ADC.SchDoc · PDF page 11</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-adc-clock.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-fpga-lvds\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">04 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-fpga-lvds\">FPGA ADC LVDS inputs</h3><p>The ADC-facing ECP5 banks are assigned differential data pairs and clock pairs. LVDS carries each signal as a voltage difference between two wires, and the accompanying clock provides the timing reference for capturing the samples. This interface forms the link between conversion and the FPGA’s intended acquisition logic. The schematic also includes the core, auxiliary, and bank-supply decoupling.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-lvds.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-lvds.webp\" alt=\"Lattice ECP5 FPGA schematic showing ADC LVDS input pairs, clock pairs, bank supplies, and core power decoupling\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>FPGA_ADC.SchDoc · PDF page 15</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-lvds.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-fpga-ftdi\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">05 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-fpga-ftdi\">FPGA FTDI LVCMOS interface</h3><p>On the host side, the FPGA connects to the FTDI bridge through a parallel LVCMOS interface. FT_D0 through FT_D31 form the data bus, with FT_CLK providing its clock connection across banks 6 and 7. This is the intended path for moving acquired data out of the FPGA toward USB. The bank supplies and local decoupling are shown alongside the signal assignments.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-ftdi.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-ftdi.webp\" alt=\"ECP5 FPGA FTDI parallel LVCMOS interface schematic with FT_D0 through FT_D31, FT_CLK, and bank decoupling\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>FPGA_FTDI.SchDoc · PDF page 16</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-ftdi.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>\n<section class=\"scope-schematic\" aria-labelledby=\"scope-usb-ftdi\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">06 / SCHEMATIC</p><h3 id=\"scope-usb-ftdi\">FTDI bridge and USB port</h3><p>The USB-C connector, HD3SS3220 orientation switch, and FT601Q bridge form the connection to the computer. The switch routes the SuperSpeed pairs for the connector orientation, while the FT601Q links USB to the FPGA’s parallel data bus. The sheet includes the transfer-control signals, bridge power connections, and a local 30 MHz crystal, separate from the ADC’s 250 MHz sampling clock.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-usb-ftdi.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/oscilloscope-usb-ftdi.webp\" alt=\"USB-C host interface schematic showing connector, HD3SS3220 orientation switch, FT601Q bridge, 30 MHz crystal, and FPGA parallel bus\" width=\"2200\" height=\"1424\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption><span>Host_USB.SchDoc · PDF page 20</span><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-usb-ftdi.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open full-resolution schematic ↗</a></figcaption></figure></section>",
    "storyTitle": "Working on the oscilloscope team",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I’m working on this oscilloscope with a team through the IEEE-run APE DeCal at UC Berkeley.</p><h3>Connecting analog and digital design</h3><p>Our project brings together the probe front end, programmable offset, ADC clocking, FPGA acquisition, and USB transfer. Working on a shared instrument means considering how a decision in one stage affects the next: the analog output must suit the ADC input, and the digital interfaces must carry the resulting samples through to the host.</p><h3>Work in progress</h3><p>We are developing the schematics and PCB integration as a team. Board bring-up and measurements will be the next steps in checking the complete signal chain.</p><h3>Learning through the APE DeCal</h3><p>I’ve found the course especially helpful and interesting because it connects circuit decisions with the practical work of building a board. In the first few weeks, we implement an advanced design to become comfortable with Altium schematics and PCB layout. That foundation carries into our team project, where the circuit and its physical implementation have to develop together.</p><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://ape.berkie.ee/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Explore the APE DeCal ↗</a><figure class=\"detail-figure cory-hall-photo\"><a href=\"assets/cory-hall-ape.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/cory-hall-ape.jpg\" alt=\"Altium schematic and PCB layout work on laptops at an electronics bench in Cory Hall, UC Berkeley\" width=\"2400\" height=\"1668\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Cory Hall, UC Berkeley.</figcaption></figure>"
},
  "cpu": {
    "label": "COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "32-bit RISC-V CPU",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">A 32-bit single-cycle RISC-V processor in progress, developed in Logisim-evolution. The schematics document the ALU, register file, and an initial top-level datapath connecting instruction handling, computation, and register write-back.</p><h3>Tools and skills</h3><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>Combinational logic</h4><p>Arithmetic and bitwise circuits, shifts, comparisons, multiplexers, decoders, and bus routing.</p></div><div><h4>State and control</h4><p>Register addressing, clocked writes, write-enable control, dual read ports, and inspection outputs.</p></div><div><h4>Computer architecture</h4><p>32-bit operands, RISC-V register conventions, instruction fields, and the planned add-immediate execution path.</p></div><div><h4>Design inspection</h4><p>Signal naming, schematic organization, bit-width consistency, and visibility into intermediate outputs.</p></div></div><h3>1. Arithmetic logic unit</h3><p>The ALU accepts two 32-bit inputs, A and B, and a 4-bit ALUSel control signal. Parallel arithmetic and logic circuits feed a result multiplexer, separating the generation of candidate values from selection of the final ALUResult.</p><p>The screenshot shows paths for addition, subtraction, AND, OR, XOR, logical left and right shifts, arithmetic right shift, signed less-than comparison, multiplication and high-product variants, and direct selection of operand B. The shift circuitry uses the low five bits of the shift operand, consistent with selecting a shift amount for a 32-bit word.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/cpu-alu.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/cpu-alu.png\" alt=\"Logisim ALU circuit with arithmetic, bitwise, shift, compare, and multiply paths\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>ALU schematic. Open the image to inspect the full-resolution circuit.</figcaption></figure><h3>2. Register-file architecture</h3><p>The register-file design targets 32 registers containing 32-bit values. Two read indices independently select source operands through separate multiplexers. A write index is decoded to select a destination, with WriteData, RegWEn, and the clock forming the write interface.</p><p>Dedicated inspection outputs for ra, sp, t0–t2, s0–s1, and a0 expose selected architectural registers without replacing the two general read ports. These outputs make processor state easier to inspect during development.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/cpu-register-file.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/cpu-register-file.png\" alt=\"Register file with write-selection decoder, dual read multiplexers, and debug outputs\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Register-file schematic and selected register inspection outputs.</figcaption></figure><h3>3. Top-level processor datapath</h3><p>The top-level schematic brings the component circuits together around an initial immediate-arithmetic execution path. A clocked program-counter register feeds the instruction-memory address interface, with an adder providing the next sequential address, PC + 4.</p><p>The instruction bus is split into opcode, destination register (rd), funct3, source register (rs1), and a 12-bit immediate field. An immediate-generator subcircuit produces the ALU’s immediate operand. The register file supplies ReadData1 to ALU input A, while the immediate feeds input B; ALUResult returns to the register file’s WriteData input.</p><p>In the earlier screenshot, the visible controls are simplified: register writes are enabled with a constant, the second read index is fixed, and the ALU selection input is not visibly connected. This documents an initial integration stage toward add-immediate (addi), with broader instruction control and execution validation still to be completed.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/cpu-datapath.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/cpu-datapath.png\" alt=\"Top-level CPU schematic showing PC plus four, instruction fields, immediate generator, register file, and ALU write-back path\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Top-level CPU integration: instruction fetch interface, field extraction, immediate generation, and register-to-ALU write-back. Open for the full-resolution schematic.</figcaption></figure><h3>Earlier integration snapshot</h3><p>ALU and register-file circuits are shown alongside an initial integrated processor datapath. Full instruction decoding, additional execution paths, and instruction-level verification remain in progress; the screenshots do not establish a fully working CPU.</p><h3>Latest integration / October 2026</h3><p>The current Logisim source brings together the ALU, register file, immediate generator, control logic, branch comparator, and partial-load/store blocks in the top-level CPU circuit. This extends the initial datapath pictured above toward instruction control and memory access.</p><p>The architecture overview below summarizes those blocks. The project remains in progress; full instruction execution and verification are ongoing.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/cpu-overview.svg\" alt=\"CPU architecture overview connecting instruction fields, control logic, registers, ALU, branch comparison, and memory access\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Architecture overview of the current source blocks. Earlier Logisim screenshots above document the initial integration.</figcaption></figure>",
    "storyTitle": "My CS 61C project",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I’m studying computer architecture in CS61C as part of my EECS studies at UC Berkeley.</p><h3>Building the pieces together</h3><p>This project is a snapshot of that work in progress. I have been developing the ALU and register file and bringing them into an initial CPU datapath, connecting the architecture I’m studying with circuits I can inspect directly.</p><p>It builds on my earlier coursework in circuits, electromagnetics, data structures, and mathematics, completed in community college. As the processor develops, I’ll continue documenting the design and its progress here.</p>"
  },
  "fusion": {
    "label": "EXPERIMENTAL PHYSICS / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "IEC plasma confinement project",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">An experimental inertial electrostatic confinement device in development, bringing together vacuum-system integration and electrode design.</p><h3>Tools and skills</h3><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>System integration</h4><p>Vacuum chamber layout, electrode arrangement, and dual-stage pumping.</p></div><div><h4>Experimental design</h4><p>Low-pressure operating conditions and electrostatic ion-acceleration geometry.</p></div></div><h3>Design scope</h3><p>The planned arrangement uses a spherical inner electrode grid at −6 kV inside a grounded outer electrode and vacuum chamber. A dual-stage pumping system is being integrated to establish low-pressure conditions for plasma experiments and electrostatic ion acceleration.</p><h3>Current status</h3><p>In progress. The current materials describe development and integration; measured fusion performance is a future milestone.</p>",
    "storyTitle": "My work in experimental physics",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">Alongside my studies at Berkeley, I’m developing this IEC project as a way to explore my interests in electrical engineering, nuclear energy, and physics.</p><p>It is still in progress. For now, the work centers on bringing the individual parts of an experimental system together. This section will grow with the project as there is more to document.</p>"
  },
  "bytes": {
    "label": "SOFTWARE & ENTREPRENEURSHIP / MAY 2021–APR 2025",
    "title": "Software and automation at Bytes",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I co-founded Bytes.io in May 2021 and helped build the company from zero. My primary contribution was technical: designing and implementing the software, data infrastructure, automation, and internal tools that supported the platform through April 2025.</p><section class=\"bytes-explainer\" aria-labelledby=\"bytes-explainer-title\"><h3 id=\"bytes-explainer-title\">What Bytes does</h3><p>Bytes helps brick-and-mortar restaurants reach more customers through online delivery platforms. We turn categories from an existing restaurant’s menu into focused virtual brands, each with its own name, menu, and product presentation. A kitchen serving a broad menu can offer separate storefronts for breakfast burritos, pizza, or chicken using the food it already prepares.</p><p>Customers order from those storefronts, and the same restaurant kitchen prepares the food. Bytes supports the storefront setup and operations; the software I built made menu creation, reporting, and reconciliation easier to manage as the network grew.</p><div class=\"bytes-explainer-images\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-virtual-brands.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-virtual-brands.jpg\" alt=\"Bytes explainer showing several focused virtual restaurant brands created from one restaurant’s menu\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>One kitchen, multiple online storefronts.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-menu-presentation.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-menu-presentation.jpg\" alt=\"Bytes example comparing a focused branded menu with the original restaurant menu on a delivery platform\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Focused menus with clear product photos.</figcaption></figure></div></section><h3>Tools and skills</h3><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>Software and AI</h4><p>Python, GCP, GPT-3.5 prompting, inventory parsing, and automated menu generation.</p></div><div><h4>Data infrastructure</h4><p>PostgreSQL on AWS RDS, relational data modeling, Pandas ETL, and AWS Lambda.</p></div><div><h4>Automation and monitoring</h4><p>EventBridge triggers, order-data checks, Colab reporting, and report distribution.</p></div><div><h4>Record matching</h4><p>Jaccard similarity, cross-system reconciliation, and low-confidence match review.</p></div></div><h3>1. AI-assisted menu creation</h3><p>In 2023, I engineered a Python and GCP workflow that parsed restaurant inventory and used GPT-3.5 prompting to generate themed virtual concepts. The tool reduced onboarding time by 97% and generated more than 1,000 storefronts, converting a manual operational bottleneck into a repeatable software pipeline.</p><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://github.com/hashemt02/menuGen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Explore menuGen source code ↗</a><h3>2. Centralized data and reporting</h3><p>I designed a relational PostgreSQL system on AWS RDS that unified six fragmented data sources. Python and Pandas ETL jobs running through Lambda automated data processing and supported analytics for more than 2,000 storefronts.</p><p>I also built Colab tools for querying store-level metrics, distributing reports, and sending region-wide updates. EventBridge and Lambda monitoring checked critical order data so operational issues could be detected sooner.</p><h3>3. Financial reconciliation</h3><p>I built a reconciliation tool that matched storefront records across marketplaces, databases, and order systems using Jaccard similarity. Low-confidence matches were routed for human review, reducing audit time by more than 90% without hiding ambiguous records.</p><a class=\"button\" href=\"https://trybytes.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Visit Bytes ↗</a>",
    "storyTitle": "Co-founding and building Bytes",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I co-founded Bytes in May 2021 and helped take it from an early idea to an operating restaurant-technology platform through April 2025.</p><h3>My contributions as a co-founder</h3><p>As a co-founder, I worked across the full path from problem definition to deployed systems. I translated operational needs into software, built the data foundation, automated high-volume workflows, and maintained the tools that the business relied on as it grew.</p><h3>Hiring and building the team</h3><p>Once we had our first ten clients, the next challenge was giving the company room to grow. I defined roles, documented how the work got done, and hired our first employee. I remember feeling the weight of that decision: someone else was choosing to build with us. It pushed me to think more carefully about how I communicated expectations and supported the people doing the work.</p><p>As those early hires became a team, I learned to pay attention to what each person brought to it. I wanted people to have a clear part of the company they could shape and feel proud of. Working through decisions together and seeing colleagues take pride in our progress became some of the most meaningful parts of building Bytes. That experience shaped how I approach engineering teams: make the work understandable, listen to different perspectives, and give people room to take ownership.</p><h3>Representing Bytes</h3><p>This photograph from a Bytes networking event captures another part of co-founding the company: representing the work, learning from operators, and building relationships around the platform.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-networking.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-networking.jpg\" alt=\"Hashem talking with attendees at a Bytes networking event\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>At a Bytes networking event.</figcaption></figure><h3>Delicious Bytes events</h3><p>The Delicious Bytes booth brought the platform into a physical event setting, presenting the restaurant-growth model directly to prospective operators and partners.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-delicious-booth.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-delicious-booth.jpg\" alt=\"Delicious Bytes event booth with branded backdrop, restaurant-growth banner, and printed materials\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Delicious Bytes booth and event materials.</figcaption></figure><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://trybytes.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Visit Bytes ↗</a><h3>Bytes team and events</h3><figure class=\"detail-figure detail-video\"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload=\"metadata\" poster=\"assets/bytes-video.jpg\" aria-label=\"Silent video from Bytes\"><source src=\"assets/bytes-video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">Your browser does not support this video.</video><figcaption>A short look at Bytes. <button type=\"button\" class=\"text-link\" data-video-toggle>Pause video</button></figcaption></figure>"
  }
};

